tel 1.0

base archimedes
import soundness

info   mathematical markup as typed values: MathML trees, the Ergo shorthand, and rendering to XML, HTML and text
detail
    _Archimedes_ models mathematical notation as an immutable tree of typed values: the
    elements of [MathML](https://www.w3.org/TR/MathML-Core/) — rows, fractions, scripts,
    roots, tables — each a case class under the `Mathml` object, with a `Math` root which
    renders to XML (`in[Xml]`), embeds in honeycomb HTML (`html`) and draws to monospaced
    text for a terminal (`draw`). MathML text reads back with `read[Math]`, and a round trip
    is lossless: attributes the model does not interpret are preserved.

    Because MathML is unbearable to write by hand, the library provides **Ergo**, a one-line
    shorthand with one glyph per structural role (`↗` superscript, `↘` subscript, `/`
    fraction, `√` root, `⋱` matrix, …) which parses to the same tree with `Ergo.parse` and
    serializes back with `Ergo.serialize`. The `ergo"…"` interpolator checks a literal while
    the code compiles and splices in any value which is `Encodable in Math`: numbers, text,
    rationals, complex numbers, vectors, matrices and dimensioned quantities all have
    encoders, reached as `value.math` (a `<math>` root) or `value.mathml` (a single node).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import Mathml.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

    val quadratic: Math = ergo"(x = (-b ± √(b↗2 - 4 a c))/(2 a))"
    quadratic.in[Xml].show      // <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>x</mi>…
    quadratic.draw              // the formula as box-drawing text, the root with a vinculum
    Math(Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2"))).in[Xml].show
    t"""<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>7</mn></math>""".read[Math]
    ```

    The element types live inside the `Mathml` object (`Mathml.Mi`, `Mathml.Mrow`, …) rather
    than flat in the package, so that generic names such as `Token`, `Layout` and `Ms` do
    not collide with other modules in the `soundness` namespace; `import Mathml.*` brings the
    bare names in where MathML is authored by hand. Reading MathML needs an `XmlSchema`
    (`xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema` admits any element), and the `Mathml.Error` and `Ergo.Error`
    raised by parsing need a tactic, such as `strategies.throwUnsafely`.

    The library has one source set, `archimedes.core`, which compiles for the JVM, Scala.js
    and Scala Native. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella: `Math`, `Mathml`,
    `Display`, `Ergo`, `Cell`, `mathmlNamespace` and the extensions `ergo`, `math`, `mathml`,
    `cell` and `draw`. The `html` extension which embeds a `Math` or a `Mathml` node in an
    HTML tree is honeycomb's, resolved through `Math.renderable` and `Mathml.renderable`.

refer archimedes.Math
refer archimedes.Mathml
refer archimedes.Ergo
refer archimedes.Cell

entry .Math   the `<math>` root element: a list of MathML nodes with a display mode and attributes
  detail
      A `Math` is the root of a MathML document: its `#contents` are the top-level nodes, its
      `#display` whether the browser sets it as a displayed block or in running text, and its
      `#attributes` any further attributes to write on the `<math>` element. It is built from
      nodes with `Math(nodes*)`, from an Ergo literal with `ergo"…"` or `Ergo.parse`, or from
      any value which is `Encodable in Math` with `value.math`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import Mathml.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      val math: Math = Math(List(Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2"))), Display.Block)
      math.in[Xml].show         // <math xmlns="…" display="block"><msup>…</msup></math>
      math.html                 // an Html of "math", for a honeycomb page
      math.draw                 // t"x²"
      math.in[Xml].show.read[Math] == math
      ```

      A `Math` renders four ways: `in[Xml]` (`.encodableInXml`) builds the xylophone tree, with
      the `xmlns` attribute always written; `html` (`.renderable`, through honeycomb's
      extension) embeds it as an `Html of "math"` wherever `<math>` is admissible; `draw`
      and `cell` lay it out as monospaced text; and a `Document[Math]` is `Showable` as
      MathML text with an XML header (`.showable`). Write `in[Xml]`, not `encode`, for the
      XML: a `Math` is also `Encodable in Math` (the identity, `.self`), so `encode` would be
      ambiguous.

      Reading is the reverse: `text.read[Math]` and `data.load[Math]` parse the XML with
      xylophone and then map the tree with `Mathml.Parser`, raising `Mathml.Error` when the
      root is not `<math>` or an element is unknown. Both need an `XmlSchema` in scope.

      `Math` is `Documentary`, with `Encoding` as its `#Metadata`, so a `Document[Math]`
      carries the encoding the file declared.
  refer archimedes.Mathml
  refer archimedes.Ergo
  refer archimedes.Display
  entry .apply   a `<math>` root over the given nodes, as `Math(nodes*)`
      The varargs constructor from `Mathml.Container`: `Math(Mi(t"x"), Mo(t"+"), Mn(t"1"))`.
      The case-class constructor, `Math(contents, display, attributes)`, takes the list
      directly with an optional `Display` and extra attributes.
  entry .aggregable   reads `Math` from MathML text, as `text.read[Math]`
      Parses the text as XML through xylophone's own `Aggregable`, then decodes the `<math>`
      root with `Mathml.Parser.decodeMath`. It needs an `XmlSchema` in scope, and tactics for
      `Parse.Error`, `Xml.Error` and `Mathml.Error`: the first two for malformed XML, the
      last when the root element is not `<math>` or an element is not MathML.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      t"""<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>x</mi></math>""".read[Math]
      ```
  entry .loadable   loads a `Document[Math]` from MathML text, with its declared encoding
      As `.aggregable`, but through xylophone's `Loadable`, keeping the XML header: the
      result is a `Document[Math]` whose metadata is the declared `Encoding` (UTF-8 when none
      is declared). It needs an `XmlSchema` and the same three tactics.
  entry .loadableData   loads a `Document[Math]` from bytes, as `data.load[Math]`
      The byte form of `.loadable`: the XML is parsed from the `Data` directly, so the
      declared encoding is honoured. It needs a `Buffering` in scope as well as an `XmlSchema`
      and the tactics for `Parse.Error`, `Xml.Error` and `Mathml.Error`. This is the instance
      a file read goes through: `path.load[Math]` gives the `Document[Math]`, whose `root` is
      the `Math`.
  entry .showable   serializes a `Document[Math]` as MathML text with an XML header
      Only a `Document[Math]` is `Showable`, not a bare `Math`: the rendering is a complete
      document, an `<?xml version="1.0" …?>` header naming the document's encoding followed
      by the `<math>` element. For the element alone, write `math.in[Xml].show`.
  entry .encodableInXml   the `<math>` element as a xylophone `Xml`, as `math.in[Xml]`
      Builds the `Xml.Element` named `math` with the `xmlns` attribute, the `display`
      attribute when `#display` is set, then `#attributes`, over each content node's own
      `in[Xml]`. Named `encodableInXml` rather than `encodable` because a `Math` is also
      `Encodable in Math` through `.self`; `in[Xml]` selects this one unambiguously.
  entry .renderable   a `Math` as honeycomb HTML, as `math.html`, where `<math>` is admissible
      The `Renderable` given with `Form = "math"`: honeycomb's `html` extension renders the
      `Math` as the WHATWG `<math>` element with the same attributes as `.encodableInXml`
      writes, and each node as foreign content through `Mathml.renderable`. The result is an
      `Html of "math"`, accepted wherever honeycomb admits embedded, phrasing or flow content.
      `Mathml.Reader.read` extracts it again from the page.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import Mathml.*

      Math(Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2"))).html.show   // <math xmlns="…"><msup>…</msup></math>
      ```
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of the node tree in bracketed prefix form
      `Math(○ ⟨mn t"2"⟩)`: the display (`○` for unset, `｢block｣` when set), then each node
      as its element name, attributes and either its text or its children, on one line.
      This is the form to read a parse or layout bug from; `show` is only for a
      `Document[Math]`.
  entry .int   an `Int` as a `<mn>` number
      `42.math` is `<math><mn>42</mn></math>`, and `42.mathml` the bare `<mn>`. `Long`,
      `Short` and `Byte` encode the same way through `.long`, `.short` and `.byte`.
  entry .long   a `Long` as a `<mn>` number
  entry .short   a `Short` as a `<mn>` number
  entry .byte   a `Byte` as a `<mn>` number
  entry .double   a `Double` as a `<mn>` number, in its `toString` form
      `3.14.mathml` is `<mn>3.14</mn>`; the text is the `Double`'s own rendering, so `5.0`
      stays `5.0`.
  entry .float   a `Float` as a `<mn>` number, in its `toString` form
  entry .bigInt   a `BigInt` as a `<mn>` number
  entry .bigDecimal   a `BigDecimal` as a `<mn>` number
  entry .text   a `Text` as `<mtext>`
      `t"speed".mathml` is `<mtext>speed</mtext>`: literal text set in the formula, not an
      identifier. For an identifier, build `Mathml.Mi` directly.
  entry .identical   any `Mathml` node as a `<math>` root over that one node
      Lets a node already in hand be used wherever an `Encodable in Math` is wanted, in
      particular as an `ergo"…"` substitution: `ergo"(x↗$node)"`.
  entry .self   a `Math` as itself: the identity encoder
      Lets a `Math` be substituted into another `ergo"…"` literal, where `Mathml.atom`
      collapses it to one node (its single content node, or an `<mrow>` of several). Because
      of this instance, `encode` on a `Math` is ambiguous; use `in[Xml]` for the XML.
  entry .q64   a `Q64` rational as `<mfrac>`, or `<mn>` when whole, with the sign hoisted
      `Q64(-3, 4).math` is `<mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow>`: the
      sign is written as a minus operator before an unsigned fraction, a whole value collapses
      to a plain `<mn>`, and a zero denominator renders as the text `NaR`. `.q32` is the
      same for `Q32`.
  entry .q32   a `Q32` rational as `<mfrac>`, or `<mn>` when whole, with the sign hoisted
  entry .QuantityEncodable   the class behind `.quantity`: an encoder for one `Quantity[units]`
      A public class only because the inline given `.quantity` inlines a reference to it; it
      wraps a pure function from a `Quantity[units]` to a `Math` as an `Encodable in Math`.
      Not meant to be constructed directly.
  entry .quantity   a dimensioned `Quantity` as its magnitude followed by its units
      `(5*Metre/Second).mathml` is the number `<mn>5.0</mn>` followed by `<mi>m</mi>` and
      `<msup><mi>s</mi><mn>-1</mn></msup>`, joined by invisible-times operators: each unit
      is an identifier, raised to its power when that is not 1, in alphabetical order of
      symbol. An inline given, so it is resolved for each concrete `units` type.
  entry .complex   a `Complex` number as `re + im i`
      `Complex(3, 4).mathml` is `<mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow>`; the
      component type needs its own `Encodable in Math`.
  entry .vector   a mosquito `Vector` as a bracketed column
      Each element is a row of an `<mtable>` inside stretchy parentheses:
      `Vector(1, 2, 3).mathml` is `(` over a column of `<mtr><mtd><mn>…</mn></mtd></mtr>`.
      The element type needs its own `Encodable in Math`.
  entry .matrix   a mosquito `Matrix` as a bracketed table
      Each row of the matrix is an `<mtr>` of `<mtd>` cells inside stretchy square
      brackets: `Matrix[2, 2]((1, 2), (3, 4)).mathml`. The element type needs its own
      `Encodable in Math`.
  entry .interpolable   the `ergo"…"` interpolator, checked while the code compiles
      The `Interpolable` given which makes `ergo"…"` a `Math`. It is in the `Math` companion,
      so no import is needed beyond `import soundness.*`; the `ergo` extension on
      `StringContext` is what names it. A malformed literal fails compilation at the character
      where parsing stopped, and a `$`-substitution must be `Encodable in Math`.
  entry #contents   the top-level nodes of the formula, in order
  entry #display   `Display.Block`, `Display.Inline`, or `Unset` to leave the attribute off
      Decides whether the browser sets the formula as a displayed equation or within running
      text. Read back from the `display` attribute by `Mathml.Parser.decodeMath`, where an
      unrecognised value becomes `Inline`.
  entry #attributes   further attributes written on the `<math>` element, as name–value pairs
      Any attribute other than `xmlns` and `display`, which are held structurally. The
      parser puts every other attribute of the root here, so a round trip keeps them.
  entry #attributePairs   every attribute the `<math>` element is written with
      `xmlns` first, then `display` if set, then `#attributes`: the list both `in[Xml]` and
      `html` write.
  entry #Self   `Math`
  entry #Metadata   `Encoding`: what a `Document[Math]` carries, the declared character encoding

entry .Mathml   the base type of every MathML node, and the home of all the element types
  detail
      Every element of Presentation MathML is an immutable case class nested in the `Mathml`
      object — `Mathml.Mi`, `Mathml.Mn`, `Mathml.Mrow`, `Mathml.Mfrac`, `Mathml.Msup` and the
      rest — and `Mathml` is the trait they all extend. A node knows its tag (`#label`), its
      ordered `#attributes` bag, and either its character data (`#text`, for a token element
      such as `<mi>`) or its child `#contents` (for a layout element such as `<mrow>`). The
      attributes are preserved verbatim, so a parse–serialize round trip is lossless even for
      an attribute the model gives no typed accessor to.

      The elements are grouped by their MathML class as the sealed traits `Token`, `Layout`,
      `Script`, `Tabular`, `Elementary` and `Semantic`; nothing dispatches on these, and code
      matches on the concrete elements. The types are nested rather than exported flat so
      that names such as `Token`, `Layout` and `Ms` do not collide with other modules in the
      `soundness` namespace: `import Mathml.*` brings the bare names in.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import Mathml.*

      val square: Mathml = Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2"))
      square.in[Xml].show            // <msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup>
      square.draw                    // t"x²"
      Mi(t"x", List(t"mathvariant" -> t"italic")).in[Xml].show
      Mrow(Mi(t"a"), Mo(t"+"), Mfrac(Mn(t"1"), Mi(t"b")))
      ```

      A node renders to two document models: `in[Xml]` (`.encodable`) builds a xylophone
      tree, and honeycomb's `html` extension (`.renderable`) builds a foreign-element tree for
      embedding in a page. Both are generic over every subtype, so `Msup(…).in[Xml]` works
      without a widening. `draw` and `cell` lay a node out as monospaced text. The uniform
      containers (`Mrow`, `Msqrt`, `Mtable`, …) each have a varargs `apply` from `Container`;
      the positional schemata (`Mfrac`, `Msub`, `Mroot`, …) name their children instead.

      `Mathml.Parser` decodes xylophone XML into nodes, `Mathml.Reader` finds and decodes a
      `<math>` subtree in honeycomb HTML, and both raise `Mathml.Error` for an element which
      is not MathML.
  refer archimedes.Math
  refer archimedes.Ergo
  refer archimedes.Cell
  entry .encodable   any node as a xylophone `Xml` element, as `node.in[Xml]`
      Generic over every `node <: Mathml`: an `Xml.Element` with the node's `label`, its
      attributes, and either a single text child or the children's own `in[Xml]`. The
      `<math>` root is `Math.encodableInXml`.
  entry .renderable   any node as honeycomb foreign content, as `node.html`
      The `Renderable` given with `Form = "#foreign"`, generic over every `node <: Mathml`:
      honeycomb's `html` extension renders the node as a foreign element with its label and
      attributes, which is how `Math.renderable` renders a formula's contents inside `<math>`.
  entry .atom   collapses a `<math>` root to one node: its single content, or an `<mrow>`
      `Mathml.atom(math)` is `math.contents` when that is exactly one node, and otherwise an
      `Mrow` of the contents. This is what the `mathml` extension and the `ergo"…"`
      interpolator use to embed an encoded value where a single operand is wanted.
  entry .Container   the varargs constructor shared by every element whose children are a list
      A trait a companion extends as `object Mrow extends Container(new Mrow(_))`, giving it
      `apply(children: Mathml*)`. `Math` extends it too, so `Math(nodes*)` builds a root.
    entry #apply   builds the element over the given children, in order
  entry .Token   the leaf elements, whose content is character data: `Mi`, `Mn`, `Mo`, `Mtext`, `Ms`, `Mspace`, `Mglyph`
      A sealed trait with no `#contents`. `Mspace` and `Mglyph` carry no text either, being
      controlled entirely by their attributes.
  entry .Mi   `<mi>`: an identifier, such as a variable or a function name
      `Mi(t"x")`. In Ergo a run of letters is one identifier, so `sin` is `<mi>sin</mi>` and
      `x y` is two. A single-letter identifier is italicised by MathML's own rules; the
      `mathvariant="normal"` attribute (Ergo's `⦱` directive) makes it upright.
    entry #value   the identifier's text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mn   `<mn>`: a numeric literal
      `Mn(t"2")`. The text is whatever the number should read as; the `Encodable in Math`
      instances for numbers write their `show` or `toString` form here.
    entry #value   the number's text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mo   `<mo>`: an operator, fence or separator
      `Mo(t"+")`. Brackets, integrals and the big operators `∑` and `∏` are operators which
      `Cell` stretches to the height of their operand when drawn; a stretchy fence in MathML
      is an `Mo` with `stretchy="true"`.
    entry #value   the operator's text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mtext   `<mtext>`: literal text set within the formula
      `Mtext(t"speed")`: words rather than mathematics. `Text` encodes to this through
      `Math.text`. It has no Ergo form, so `Ergo.serialize` rejects it.
    entry #value   the text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Ms   `<ms>`: a string literal, shown in quotation marks
    entry #value   the string's text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mspace   `<mspace>`: blank space of a given width, height or depth
      `Mspace(List(t"width" -> t"1em"))` renders as `<mspace width="1em"/>`. It has no text
      and no children; `Cell` draws it as one blank column.
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mglyph   `<mglyph>`: a glyph from an image, by its attributes
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Layout   the general layout schemata: `Mrow`, `Mfrac`, `Msqrt`, `Mroot`, `Mstyle`, `Merror`, `Mpadded`, `Mphantom`, `Menclose`, `Mfenced`
      A sealed trait of elements with children and no text.
  entry .Mrow   `<mrow>`: a horizontal row of nodes, grouped as one unit
      `Mrow(Mi(t"x"), Mo(t"+"), Mn(t"1"))`. The grouping element: an Ergo bracket group is an
      `Mrow`, and `Mathml.atom` wraps several nodes in one. `Cell` draws a row by aligning the
      baselines of its children and spacing binary operators.
    entry .apply   an `<mrow>` over the given children, as `Mrow(children*)`
    entry #contents   the row's children, in order
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mfrac   `<mfrac>`: a fraction, numerator over denominator
      `Mfrac(Mn(t"1"), Mn(t"2"))`; Ergo's `a/b`. `linethickness="0"` (Ergo's `═(0)`) removes
      the bar, for a binomial coefficient.
    entry #numerator   the node above the bar
    entry #denominator   the node below the bar
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msqrt   `<msqrt>`: a square root over its children
      `Msqrt(Mi(t"x"))`; Ergo's `√x`. `Cell` draws a vinculum over the radicand.
    entry .apply   an `<msqrt>` over the given children, as `Msqrt(children*)`
    entry #contents   the radicand, as a list of nodes
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mroot   `<mroot>`: an nth root, with the index drawn above the sign
      `Mroot(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"3"))` is the cube root of `x`: the base first, then the index,
      as MathML orders them. In Ergo an index atom written immediately before `√` gives one:
      `3√x`.
    entry #base   the radicand
    entry #index   the degree of the root
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mstyle   `<mstyle>`: a style change applied to its children
    entry .apply   an `<mstyle>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Merror   `<merror>`: content marked as erroneous
    entry .apply   an `<merror>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mpadded   `<mpadded>`: children with adjusted size or position
    entry .apply   an `<mpadded>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mphantom   `<mphantom>`: children which take space but are not drawn
      `Cell` draws it as a blank block of the children's size.
    entry .apply   an `<mphantom>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Menclose   `<menclose>`: children enclosed by a notation such as a box or strike
      MathML 3 only: MathML Core dropped it, so it has no Ergo directive.
    entry .apply   a `<menclose>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mfenced   `<mfenced>`: children between a pair of fences
      `Cell` draws it with parentheses stretched to the height of the content, which is how
      a tall expression is bracketed in text output; in MathML Core the equivalent is an
      `Mrow` of stretchy `Mo` brackets, which is what the `Math.vector` and `Math.matrix`
      encoders write.
    entry .apply   an `<mfenced>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Script   the script and limit schemata: `Msub`, `Msup`, `Msubsup`, `Munder`, `Mover`, `Munderover`, `Mmultiscripts`, `Mprescripts`, `Mnone`
      A sealed trait. The corner scripts and the centred limits are positional; `Mmultiscripts`
      is a container whose children interleave the base, its postscripts, an `Mprescripts`
      marker and its prescripts, with `Mnone` as an empty placeholder.
  entry .Msub   `<msub>`: a base with a subscript
      `Msub(Mi(t"x"), Mi(t"i"))`; Ergo's `x↘i`. `Cell` sets a subscript on the base's own
      line in Unicode subscript glyphs (`xᵢ`) when every character has one, and stacks it
      below otherwise.
    entry #base   the node the script attaches to
    entry #subscript   the script, set below right
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msup   `<msup>`: a base with a superscript
      `Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2"))`; Ergo's `x↗2`. `Cell` draws it as `x²` when the script is
      spellable in Unicode superscript glyphs, and stacks it diagonally above otherwise.
    entry #base   the node the script attaches to
    entry #superscript   the script, set above right
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msubsup   `<msubsup>`: a base with both a subscript and a superscript
      Ergo's `x↘i↗2`: a base which takes one `↘` and one `↗` merges into this element.
    entry #base   the node the scripts attach to
    entry #subscript   the lower script
    entry #superscript   the upper script
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Munder   `<munder>`: a base with an underscript or lower limit
      Ergo's `x↓n`. When the underscript is a single `Mo`, the Ergo parser marks it
      `accentunder="true"`, treating a bar or arrow as a tight accent rather than a limit.
    entry #base   the node the script attaches to
    entry #underscript   the script, centred below
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mover   `<mover>`: a base with an overscript or upper limit
      Ergo's `x↑n`, or `x↑^` for a hat, which the parser marks `accent="true"` because the
      overscript is a lone operator.
    entry #base   the node the script attaches to
    entry #overscript   the script, centred above
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Munderover   `<munderover>`: a base with limits both below and above
      Ergo's `∑↓(i = 1)↑n`: the natural form for a large operator's limits. `Cell` draws
      the limits centred above and below the base.
    entry #base   the node the scripts attach to
    entry #underscript   the lower limit
    entry #overscript   the upper limit
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mmultiscripts   `<mmultiscripts>`: a base with any number of pre- and postscripts
      The children are the base, then subscript–superscript pairs after it, then an
      `Mprescripts` marker and pairs before it; `Mnone` fills an empty position.
    entry .apply   an `<mmultiscripts>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the base, scripts and markers, in MathML's order
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mprescripts   `<mprescripts/>`: the marker in `Mmultiscripts` after which the prescripts follow
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mnone   `<mnone/>`: an empty script position in `Mmultiscripts`
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Tabular   the table schemata: `Mtable`, `Mtr`, `Mlabeledtr`, `Mtd`, `Maligngroup`, `Malignmark`
      A sealed trait. MathML Core keeps the table elements but none of their MathML 3
      attributes (`columnalign`, `rowspan`, `frame`, …), so those have no Ergo directives.
  entry .Mtable   `<mtable>`: a table or matrix of rows
      `Mtable(Mtr(Mtd(Mn(t"1")), Mtd(Mn(t"2"))))`. Ergo's `⋱`, `⋯` and `⋮` introducers build
      a matrix, a one-row table and a one-column table; the `Math.vector` and `Math.matrix`
      encoders write one inside stretchy brackets.
    entry .apply   an `<mtable>` over the given rows
    entry #contents   the rows, each an `Mtr` or `Mlabeledtr`
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mtr   `<mtr>`: a table row of cells
    entry .apply   an `<mtr>` over the given cells
    entry #contents   the cells, each an `Mtd`
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mlabeledtr   `<mlabeledtr>`: a table row whose first child is its label
    entry .apply   an `<mlabeledtr>` over the given label and cells
    entry #contents   the label, then the cells
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mtd   `<mtd>`: one cell of a table row
    entry .apply   an `<mtd>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the cell's content
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Maligngroup   `<maligngroup/>`: an alignment group marker within a cell
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Malignmark   `<malignmark/>`: an alignment point marker within a cell
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Elementary   the elementary-maths schemata: `Mstack`, `Mlongdiv`, `Msgroup`, `Msrow`, `Mscarries`, `Mscarry`, `Msline`
      A sealed trait of the MathML 3 elements for column arithmetic and long division, which
      MathML Core dropped; they parse and serialize, but have no Ergo form.
  entry .Mstack   `<mstack>`: a column-aligned stack of rows for elementary arithmetic
    entry .apply   an `<mstack>` over the given rows
    entry #contents   the rows
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mlongdiv   `<mlongdiv>`: a long division layout
    entry .apply   an `<mlongdiv>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the divisor, result, dividend and the rows beneath
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msgroup   `<msgroup>`: a group of rows within a stack, shifted together
    entry .apply   an `<msgroup>` over the given rows
    entry #contents   the rows
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msrow   `<msrow>`: one row of a stack
    entry .apply   an `<msrow>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the row's digits and operators
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mscarries   `<mscarries>`: a row of carries above the row that follows
    entry .apply   an `<mscarries>` over the given carries
    entry #contents   the carries, each an `Mscarry` or a plain node
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Mscarry   `<mscarry>`: one carry within `Mscarries`
    entry .apply   an `<mscarry>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the carry's content
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Msline   `<msline/>`: a horizontal rule within a stack
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Semantic   `<maction>` and the semantics bridge: `Maction`, `Semantics`, `Annotation`, `AnnotationXml`
      A sealed trait. `Semantics` pairs a presentation subtree with annotations: an
      `Annotation` carries character data such as a TeX string, and an `AnnotationXml` a
      nested markup subtree.
  entry .Maction   `<maction>`: children bound to an action such as toggle or highlight
      The `actiontype` attribute (Ergo's `⚙(type)`) names the action; it is legacy, and
      undefined in MathML Core.
    entry .apply   an `<maction>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the children
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Semantics   `<semantics>`: a presentation subtree with one or more annotations
    entry .apply   a `<semantics>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the presentation node, then its annotations
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Annotation   `<annotation>`: character data annotating a formula, such as its TeX source
    entry #value   the annotation's text
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .AnnotationXml   `<annotation-xml>`: a markup subtree annotating a formula
    entry .apply   an `<annotation-xml>` over the given children
    entry #contents   the annotation's nodes
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs
  entry .Error   raised when XML or HTML does not hold a MathML tree
    detail
        Error 430: _the MathML could not be parsed because reason_. Raised by `Math.aggregable`,
        `Math.loadable`, `Math.loadableData`, `Mathml.Parser` and `Mathml.Reader` when the
        root element is not `<math>` or an element has a label which is not a MathML
        element. Well-formedness of the XML itself is xylophone's `Xml.Error` and
        `Parse.Error`, raised before this one.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        safely(t"<svg><rect/></svg>".read[Math])   // Unset: the root was <svg>
        ```

        Under `strategies.throwUnsafely` the same read throws, and the error's `#reason` is
        `Mathml.Error.Reason.NotMathml(t"svg")`.
    entry .Reason   why the tree is not MathML: `NotMathml(label)` or `UnknownElement(label)`
        `NotMathml(label)` (reason 1, _the root element was `<label>` instead of `<math>`_):
        the document's root, or the HTML page, had no `<math>` element; the label is
        `<missing>` when a fragment or a page held none at all. `UnknownElement(label)`
        (reason 2, _the element `<label>` is not a known MathML element_): an element inside
        `<math>` whose tag `Mathml.Parser.decodeNode` does not recognise.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   which fault was found
  entry .Parser   decodes a xylophone `Xml` tree into `Math` and `Mathml` nodes
    detail
        The decoder behind `read[Math]`: it dispatches on each element's label to the
        matching case class, keeping every attribute verbatim in the node's `attributes` bag,
        except `xmlns` and `display` on the root, which `Math` holds structurally. A
        positional element (`<mfrac>`, `<msup>`, …) missing a child gets an empty `Mrow` in
        its place.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        val xml: Xml = t"""<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>x</mi></math>""".read[Xml]
        val math: Math = Mathml.Parser.decodeMath(Mathml.Parser.rootElement(xml))
        ```

        Every method which decodes needs a `Tactic[Mathml.Error]`.
    entry .labelOf   the label of an `Xml` value, or `<unknown>` for a non-element
    entry .findMath   the first `<math>` element among a list of XML nodes
        Raises `Mathml.Error` with `NotMathml(<missing>)` when there is none.
    entry .rootElement   the `<math>` element of an XML value: the value itself, or the first in a fragment
        Raises `Mathml.Error` with `NotMathml(label)` when the value is an element of another
        name, or a fragment without a `<math>` element.
    entry .decodeMath   decodes a `<math>` element into a `Math`, reading `display` and the other attributes
        The `display` attribute becomes `#display` (`Display.Block`, `Display.Inline`, or
        `Inline` for any other value; `Unset` when absent); `xmlns` is dropped, since it is
        always written back; every other attribute is kept. The children are decoded with
        `.decodeNode`.
    entry .decodeNode   decodes one element into the `Mathml` case class for its label
        Raises `Mathml.Error` with `UnknownElement(label)` for a label which is not one of
        the MathML elements.
  entry .Reader   extracts MathML embedded in a honeycomb HTML tree
    detail
        Honeycomb parses `<math>` as a foreign element with its own node types, so the reader
        walks the HTML tree depth-first for the first `<math>` subtree, transcribes it into a
        xylophone `Xml.Element`, and decodes that with `Mathml.Parser`. This is the inverse
        of `math.html`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import Mathml.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val expression = Math(Mrow(Mi(t"a"), Mo(t"+"), Mn(t"1")))
        Mathml.Reader.read(expression.html) == expression
        ```
    entry .read   the first `<math>` in an HTML tree, decoded as a `Math`
        Raises `Mathml.Error` with `NotMathml(<missing>)` when the tree has no `<math>`
        element, and `UnknownElement` for a non-MathML element inside it; needs a
        `Tactic[Mathml.Error]`.
    entry .findMath   the first `<math>` element in an HTML tree, as honeycomb's own element type, or `Unset`
  entry #label   the element's tag name, such as `mi` or `annotation-xml`
  entry #attributes   the element's attributes, as name–value pairs, in order
      Preserved verbatim: an attribute the model has no accessor for still round-trips.
  entry #contents   the child nodes, in order; empty for a token element
  entry #text   the character data of a token element, or `Unset` for a layout element
      `Mspace` and `Mglyph` are tokens with no text, so theirs is `Unset` too.

entry .Display   whether a `<math>` root is a displayed block or set inline in running text
  detail
      The value of the `display` attribute on the root `<math>` element: `Display.Block` for
      a displayed equation on its own line, `Display.Inline` for one set within a paragraph.
      A `Math` holds one as its `display`, or `Unset` to leave the attribute off, and the
      Ergo directives `◻` and `▭` set it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import Mathml.*

      Math(List(Mn(t"1")), Display.Block).in[Xml].show   // …display="block"…
      Display.Block.encode                               // t"block"
      ```
  entry .encodable   renders a display mode as the attribute's text: `block` or `inline`
  entry .unapply   matches the text `block` or `inline` as a `Display`, for a pattern
      `case Display(display) =>` on a `Text`; any other text does not match, which is how
      `Mathml.Parser.decodeMath` falls back to `Inline` for an unknown value.

entry .Ergo   the Ergo shorthand: a one-line notation which parses to MathML and serializes back
  detail
      Ergo is a compact linear notation for Presentation MathML, one glyph per structural
      role, which `.parse` turns into a `Math` and `.serialize` writes back out, exactly
      inverse over the elements Ergo has syntax for. An expression is delimited by a bracket
      pair, and its **first character** chooses which of `(`, `[`, `{` or `⟨` acts as
      grouping throughout; every other bracket is then a literal operator. A grouped run is
      one `<mrow>`.

      Tokens: a run of letters is one identifier (`sin` is `<mi>sin</mi>`; `x y` is two), a
      run of digits with an interior `.` is one number, a space separates and renders as
      nothing, and any other character is an operator. The structural glyphs, tightest first:

      - `√` prefix is a square root, `<msqrt>`; an index atom immediately before it, with no
        space, makes an nth root, `<mroot>`: `3√x`.
      - `↗` and `↘` are superscript and subscript, `<msup>` and `<msub>`; a base taking one
        of each merges into `<msubsup>`.
      - `↑` and `↓` are overscript and underscript, `<mover>` and `<munder>`, merging into
        `<munderover>`; a script which is a lone operator is marked as an accent.
      - `/` is a fraction, `<mfrac>`, binding looser than the scripts.
      - Juxtaposition is a row.
      - `⋱`, `⋯` and `⋮` introduce a matrix, a row vector and a column vector from the group
        which follows: `⋱(((1)(2))((3)(4)))` is a 2×2 `<mtable>`.

      An operator glyph with a missing operand degrades to a literal operator, so `(↗)` is
      `<mo>↗</mo>` rather than an error. Each MathML Core presentation attribute has a
      **postfix directive** glyph which binds to the atom or group to its left: a boolean or
      enumerated attribute is a bare glyph (`⇿` for `stretchy="true"`, `↮` for false, `⊰`
      for `form="prefix"`, `⦱` for `mathvariant="normal"`, `◆` for `largeop`), and an
      open-valued one takes its value in the grouping bracket (`●(red)` for `mathcolor`,
      `⟑(2em)` for `mathsize`, `═(0)` for `linethickness`, `⌄(1)` for `scriptlevel`).
      Grouping decides the scope: `(x↗2)●(red)` colours the whole power, `x↗2●(red)` only
      the `2`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An identifier
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            Ergo.parse(t"(x)").draw            // x
      frame
        label A superscript
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            Ergo.parse(t"(x↗2)").draw          // x²
      frame
        label A fraction, binding looser than the script
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            Ergo.parse(t"(x↗2/2)").draw        // x² over a bar over 2
      frame
        label A square root over the fraction
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            Ergo.parse(t"(√(x↗2/2))").draw     // the root's stem and vinculum grow to the fraction's height
      frame
        label A sum with limits
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            Ergo.parse(t"(∑↓(i = 1)↑n √(x↘i↗2/2))").draw   // ∑ with n above and i = 1 below
      ##
      ```

      Parsing needs a `Tactic[Ergo.Error]`; the `ergo"…"` interpolator parses the literal
      while the code compiles instead, and reports a malformed one at the character where
      parsing stopped. Serialization rejects an element outside the Ergo subset (`<mtext>`,
      `<mspace>`, `<mstyle>`, the table attributes, the elementary-maths elements) with an
      `Ergo.Error` rather than approximating it, and drops the root's own attributes.
  refer archimedes.ergo
  refer archimedes.Math
  entry .parse   parses Ergo text into a `Math`
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Ergo.parse(t"(x = (-b ± √(b↗2 - 4 a c))/(2 a))").in[Xml].show
      Ergo.parse(t"(x↘i↗2)")   // Math(List(Msubsup(Mi(t"x"), Mi(t"i"), Mn(t"2"))))
      ```

      Raises `Ergo.Error` for an empty input, an input which does not begin with one of the
      four grouping brackets, a group which is not closed, an input which ends mid-way, or
      an introducer (`⋱`, `⋯`, `⋮`) not followed by a group. The error's `offset` is the
      character where parsing stopped.
  entry .interpolate   parses the parts of an interpolated literal, with a node spliced at each hole
      The runtime half of the `ergo"…"` interpolator: the text parts are joined with a
      placeholder at each substitution, and each placeholder parses as the corresponding
      already-encoded `Mathml` atom, in order. Needs a `Tactic[Ergo.Error]`; the interpolator
      itself calls `.unsafeInterpolate`, having checked the literal at compile time.
  entry .unsafeInterpolate   `.interpolate` without a tactic, for a literal already checked at compile time
      Takes `(using Unsafe)`: the `ergo"…"` macro's generated code calls it, since the
      literal's well-formedness was established as it compiled. Code which parses text it
      has not checked should call `.parse` or `.interpolate` and handle `Ergo.Error`.
  entry .serialize   writes a `Math` back out as Ergo text, with `(` as the grouping bracket
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Ergo.serialize(Ergo.parse(t"(x↗(y + 1))"))   // t"(x↗(y+1))"
      Ergo.serialize(Ergo.parse(t"(x●(red))"))     // t"(x●(red))"
      ```

      Insignificant spaces are dropped, and a space is kept only where two tokens would
      otherwise merge (two letters into one identifier, two digits into one number).
      Attributes are written as directives, preferring a bare glyph where the value has one;
      an accent attribute implied by a lone-operator script is omitted, since parsing
      restores it. A parsed expression serializes and re-parses to an equal tree. Raises
      `Ergo.Error` with `Unsupported(label)` for an element with no Ergo form, such as
      `<mtext>`; needs a `Tactic[Ergo.Error]`.
  entry .Error   raised when Ergo text cannot be parsed, or a tree cannot be written as Ergo
    detail
        Error 431: _the ergo expression could not be parsed because reason_. Raised by
        `Ergo.parse` and `Ergo.interpolate` for malformed text, and by `Ergo.serialize` for an
        element outside the Ergo subset. In the `ergo"…"` interpolator the same faults are
        compile errors, positioned at the character where parsing stopped (the last character
        for an unclosed group, the first for a missing opening bracket).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        safely(Ergo.parse(t"x + y"))   // Unset: no opening bracket
        ```
    entry .Reason   which fault was found
        `Empty` (1, _the expression was empty_); `BadOpener(char)` (2, _the expression must
        start with a bracket, not `char`_): the first character is not `(`, `[`, `{` or `⟨`;
        `Unclosed(expected)` (3, _a group was not closed with `expected`_); `UnexpectedEnd`
        (4, _the expression ended unexpectedly_); `MissingBody(char)` (5, _the `char`
        introducer must be followed by a group_): a `⋱`, `⋯` or `⋮` with no group after it;
        `Unsupported(label)` (6, _the `<label>` element has no ergo representation_): from
        `Ergo.serialize`. The `char` fields are `Char`s.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   which fault was found
    entry #offset   the character position at which parsing stopped, or `-1` from serialization

entry .Cell   a block of monospaced text with a baseline: the terminal rendering of a formula
  detail
      A `Cell` is a rectangle of character cells, `#width` columns by `#height` rows, with a
      `#baseline` — the row, from the top, of the mathematical axis which composition aligns
      on. Its `#lines` are `Writing`s, so a combining accent or a multi-codepoint cluster
      counts as one column, and every line has exactly `width` columns. `Cell.of` lays out
      any `Mathml` node, and `#render` flattens the block to newline-separated `Text`; the
      extensions `cell` and `draw` on a `Mathml` or a `Math` are the usual entry.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import Mathml.*

      Mfrac(Mn(t"1"), Mn(t"2")).draw          // t" 1 \n───\n 2 "
      Msup(Mi(t"x"), Mn(t"2")).draw           // t"x²"
      Msqrt(Mrow(Mi(t"x"), Mo(t"+"), Mn(t"1"))).draw   // t"┌─────\n√x + 1"
      Mfenced(Mfrac(Mn(t"1"), Mn(t"2"))).draw // t"⎛ 1 ⎞\n⎜───⎟\n⎝ 2 ⎠"
      ```

      The layout rules: a superscript or subscript which can be spelled in Unicode's
      superscript and subscript glyphs (digits, signs, brackets and much of the Latin
      alphabet, though no superscript `q`) is set on the base's own line, as `x²` or `xᵢ`;
      anything else — a Greek letter, a fraction, a nested script — stacks diagonally. A
      fraction centres its parts over a `─` rule; a square root draws a `┌─` vinculum, with
      a `│` stem and `╲` foot when the radicand is taller than a line; an nth root puts the
      index a line above the sign. In a row, binary operators are spaced, and brackets,
      integrals and the big operators `∑` and `∏` stretch to the height of the content: a
      bracket becomes box-drawing pieces, an integral a `╭`–`│`–`╯` stroke (doubled or
      tripled, with a `○` for a contour integral), and `∑` and `∏` line-art sized to the
      summand. The companion's builders (`beside`, `fraction`, `radical`, `bracket`, …) are
      the primitives `of` composes, usable directly for a layout of one's own.

      In the `soundness` umbrella the name `Cell` is this type; probably's table cell is
      `Tally`.
  refer archimedes.draw
  refer archimedes.Mathml
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering: the dimensions, the baseline and each line as a text literal
      `Cell(2×1@0 t"xy")`: width by height, `@` the baseline row, then every line quoted, so
      that trailing padding is visible. `#render` shows the block as it will appear, which an
      inspection cannot.
  entry .empty   the cell of one empty line, zero columns wide
  entry .line   a one-line cell of the given text, with baseline 0
      The width is the text's grapheme width under the wide-character metric, so a CJK
      character counts two columns.
  entry .blank   a cell of spaces, of the given width, height and baseline
  entry .beside   joins cells left to right with their baselines aligned
      The workhorse of `<mrow>`: each cell is framed with blank rows above and below so that
      every baseline falls on the same row, then the rows are concatenated. The result's
      baseline is the greatest of the inputs'.
  entry .fraction   stacks a numerator over a `─` rule over a denominator
      Both parts are centred within the wider plus a column of margin each side; the
      baseline is the rule.
  entry .superscript   sets a script cell diagonally above and to the right of a base cell
      The stacked form, used when `Cell.of` cannot spell the script in Unicode glyphs. The
      baseline moves down by the script's height.
  entry .subscript   sets a script cell diagonally below and to the right of a base cell
  entry .subsup   sets a subscript and a superscript cell to the right of a base, one above the other
      Both scripts are padded to the wider; the base's baseline is kept, shifted by the
      superscript's height.
  entry .overscript   centres a cell above a base cell
  entry .underscript   centres a cell below a base cell
  entry .underover   centres one cell above and another below a base cell
      How `Munderover` draws: a summation's limits above and below the `∑`.
  entry .radical   draws a square-root sign and vinculum around a cell
      A one-line radicand gets the `√` glyph with a `┌─…` overline; a taller one gets a
      `╲` foot and a `│` stem rising to the corner.
  entry .root   draws an nth root: the index one line above the sign, to its left
      Always the tall form of the sign, so that the index has a line to sit on.
  entry .fence   brackets a cell with an opening and a closing character, stretched to its height
      `fence('(', ')', inner)`: how `Mfenced` draws.
  entry .bracket   a bracket character stretched to a height, as a one-column cell
      A single-line height gives the literal character; taller, `(`, `[` and `{` become
      their box-drawing top, middle and bottom pieces (a brace puts its `⎨` at the baseline
      row), and any other character a `│` rule. `opening` chooses the left- or right-hand
      pieces.
  entry .integral   an integral sign stretched to a height, with a column of padding after it
      `∫`, `∬` and `∭` are one, two and three `╭`–`│`–`╯` strokes; `∮`, `∯` and `∰` overlay
      a `○` on the axis row of the first stroke. A single-line height gives the plain glyph.
  entry .bigOperator   `∑` or `∏` as line-art of a given height
      A sum is `▁` and `▔` bars joined by `╲` and `╱` diagonals stepping in to a vertex, which
      needs an even height of at least four; a product is a `┬──┬` lintel over two `│` legs,
      at any height. The baseline is the vertical middle.
  entry .row   lays out a list of nodes side by side, stretching brackets, integrals and big operators
      Measures the non-stretchy content first, then draws each stretchy operator to that
      height, and joins everything with `beside`. A one-line row keeps every operator's plain
      glyph.
  entry .of   lays out any `Mathml` node as a cell
      The recursive layout: each element kind maps to the corresponding builder, a token to
      `line` (with a binary operator spaced and an invisible operator dropped), and an
      element with no special layout to a row of its children.
  entry #lines   the rows of the block, top to bottom, each exactly `width` columns
  entry #width   the number of columns
  entry #baseline   the row, from the top, of the axis composition aligns on
  entry #height   the number of rows
  entry #render   the block as text, its lines joined with newlines
      The form to print: trailing spaces are kept, so every line is the same width.
  entry #hpad   pads each line with spaces on the left and right, widening the block
  entry #hcenter   centres the block within a wider number of columns; never narrows
  entry #framed   adds blank rows above and below to reach a given ascent and descent
      Used by `Cell.beside` to bring cells to a common height before joining them row-wise.

entry .mathmlNamespace   the MathML namespace URI, written as `xmlns` on every `<math>` root
    `http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML`: `Math.attributePairs` always puts it first.

entry .math   any `Encodable in Math` value as a `<math>` root, as `value.math`
    An extension on every type with an `Encodable in Math` instance — the numbers, `Text`,
    `Q64`/`Q32`, `Complex`, mosquito `Vector` and `Matrix`, dimensioned `Quantity`s, and any
    `Mathml` node or `Math` — giving the encoded `Math`. The instances live in the `Math`
    companion, so no import is needed.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    42.math.in[Xml].show   // <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>42</mn></math>
    Q64(-3, 4).math
    ```

entry .mathml   any `Encodable in Math` value as a single MathML node, as `value.mathml`
    As `math`, then collapsed with `Mathml.atom` to one node: the single content node, or an
    `<mrow>` of several. For building a larger tree by hand from encoded pieces.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import Mathml.*

    3.14.mathml                   // Mn(t"3.14")
    Complex(3, 4).mathml          // Mrow(Mn(t"3"), Mo(t"+"), Mn(t"4"), Mi(t"i"))
    Msup(Mi(t"x"), 2.mathml)
    ```

entry .ergo   the `ergo"…"` interpolator: an Ergo expression parsed as the code compiles
    `ergo"(x↗2)"` is a `Math`, parsed at compile time, so a malformed literal is a compile
    error positioned at the character where parsing stopped: _the ergo expression could not
    be parsed because a group was not closed with )_. A `$`-substitution may be any value
    which is `Encodable in Math` — a number, text, a `Mathml` node, another `Math`, a vector
    — and is spliced as a single atom, so a directive or a script binds to the whole of it; a
    value which is not encodable is a compile error naming its type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val exponent = 2
    val vector = Vector(1, 2, 3)
    ergo"(x↗$exponent)".in[Xml].show     // …<msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup>…
    ergo"($exponent●(red))"              // <mn mathcolor="red">2</mn>
    ergo"(A $vector)"                    // the vector as a bracketed column
    ```

    The notation is described under `Ergo`; the `Interpolable` given is `Math.interpolable`,
    in the companion, so `import soundness.*` suffices.

entry .cell   a `Mathml` node or a `Math` laid out as a `Cell`, for composing with other blocks
    `Cell.of(node)`, or for a `Math` the layout of its contents as a row. `draw` is the same
    block as text.

entry .draw   a `Mathml` node or a `Math` as monospaced, box-drawing text for a terminal
    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    Ergo.parse(t"(y = (1/2)↗2)").draw
    Math(List(Mathml.Mroot(Mathml.Mn(t"8"), Mathml.Mn(t"3")))).draw   // t"3 ┌─\n ╲│8"
    ```

    Several lines joined with newlines, every line the same width; `Cell` describes the
    layout rules.

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind the `ergo"…"` interpolator
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro has a stable home.
  entry .ergoInterpolator   the macro behind `ergo"…"`: checks the literal at compile time and splices `Ergo.unsafeInterpolate`
      Recovers the literal parts, turns each substitution into a `Mathml` atom through its
      `Encodable in Math` (a compile error if there is none), parses the whole with
      placeholder atoms so that a fault is reported at its position, then emits code which
      re-parses at runtime with the real atoms woven in.
